  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries involve negative outcomes (losses) with different probabilities. The outcomes are -4000 Euros (Lottery B) and -3000 Euros (Lottery A), with probabilities of 20% and 25%, respectively. The non-loss outcomes are 0 Euros in both cases. The decision reason considers differences between outcomes negligible, which may apply here since both involve losses (albeit different in magnitude), and focuses instead on the probability of a 'favorable outcome'. Given that both lotteries include 0 Euros as a possible result, and the context is loss avoidance, the 'favorable outcome' can be interpreted as 0 Euros (i.e., avoiding a loss). Thus, the probability of the favorable outcome (0 Euros) is 80% for Lottery B and 75% for Lottery A. The reason would prefer Lottery B, as it has a higher probability (80% > 75%) of the favorable outcome.",
